    "hints": [
      {
        "id": "exact-hint-1",
        "level": 1,
        "text": "Begin with the scope. A single dot means the current directory and everything find descends into beneath it."
      },
      {
        "id": "exact-hint-2",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "After find ., use -name followed by the exact lowercase basename mission.txt. Exact capitalization matters."
      },
      {
        "id": "exact-hint-3",
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Run: find . -name 'mission.txt'"
      },
      {
        "id": "scope-hint-1",
        "level": 1,
        "text": "Do not begin at . when the question names only one subtree. Put that subtree immediately after find."
      },
      {
        "id": "scope-hint-2",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Start at projects, require regular-file type f, then test the exact name telemetry.log."
      },
      {
        "id": "scope-hint-3",
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Run: find projects -type f -name 'telemetry.log'"
      },
      {
        "id": "type-hint-1",
        "level": 1,
        "text": "A name test alone would accept both path types. Add a type test for directories."
      },
      {
        "id": "type-hint-2",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Start at projects. The directory type letter is d; combine -type d with the exact name reports."
      },
      {
        "id": "type-hint-3",
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Run: find projects -type d -name 'reports'"
      },
      {
        "id": "pattern-hint-1",
        "level": 1,
        "text": "Use the smallest named subtree as scope, require regular files, and let find\u2014not the shell\u2014interpret the wildcard pattern."
      },
      {
        "id": "pattern-hint-2",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Start at data, use -type f, and quote a -name pattern consisting of any basename ending in lowercase .csv."
      },
      {
        "id": "pattern-hint-3",
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Run: find data -type f -name '*.csv'"
      }
    ],
    "tutor": {
      "goalSummary": "Student reads find as a bounded recursive search: choose the smallest safe starting point, combine case-sensitive -name with -type when needed, quote wildcard patterns, interpret printed paths, and leave the searched tree unchanged.",
      "misconceptions": [
        "The starting point is not decorative: find . searches the current tree, while find projects cannot return a matching path under archive.",
        "find searches recursively beneath each starting point; the student does not need to cd through every directory first.",
        "-name compares only the final path component, or basename, not the entire printed path.",
        "Plain -name is case-sensitive, so mission.txt and Mission.txt are different names and *.csv does not match SENSORS.CSV.",
        "A wildcard in a quoted -name pattern is interpreted by find; leaving it unquoted lets the shell try to expand it before find runs.",
        "-type f means regular file and -type d means directory; an extension-like name does not prove path type.",
        "find normally prints matching paths and does not change them, but actions such as -delete and -exec can be destructive or invoke other programs.",
        "Searching from / is not more correct than searching the assigned subtree; it is broader, slower, noisier, and may encounter inaccessible or sensitive paths.",
        "An empty result can correctly mean no path in the chosen scope passed every test; it does not automatically mean find failed."
      ],
      "constraints": [
        "Keep coaching to find with the exact bounded starting points ., projects, and data plus -name, -type f, and -type d.",
        "Require the student to state the intended scope before discussing name or type predicates.",
        "Ask for a prediction of included matches and excluded decoys before each command.",
        "Give hints in level order and reveal only the current task's exact command at level 3.",
        "Do not suggest /, workspace ancestors, home-directory searches, multiple starting points, -iname, -path, -maxdepth, -mindepth, logical operators, parentheses, or format actions.",
        "Never suggest -delete, -exec, -execdir, -ok, xargs, shell loops, command substitution, pipes, redirection, aliases, or command chaining in this lesson.",
        "Do not accept ls traversal, grep, shell globbing alone, or manually visiting directories as equivalent evidence for the requested recursive find search.",
        "Treat quotes as safety and correctness syntax for wildcard patterns, while recognizing that command observation records the resulting argument without quote characters.",
        "Keep all work in workspace/search-lab and reset if any fixture is changed, renamed, copied, or removed.",
        "After completion, ask the student to explain each output path in terms of starting scope, basename test, type test, and case sensitivity."
      ]
    },
